"""Interpretive text for the mid-semester report.

Written after inspecting the stored results of run main-01 (525 samples), the two retrieval
ablations (abl-topk3, abl-nocap) and the near-greedy run (lowtemp). Every figure quoted here comes from
results/<run>/summary.json or from counts made over results/<run>/samples.jsonl; if a run is repeated,
these numbers must be re-checked. Paragraphs are separated by a blank line.
"""

ABSTRACT = """Writing Infrastructure as Code (IaC) by hand is slow and error-prone, and large language models (LLMs) can draft it but often ignore organisational conventions that a plain-language request never states. This dissertation builds and evaluates a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) assistant that turns natural-language requirements into Terraform for Google Cloud, grounded in the public provider documentation and a set of organisational conventions, and checks every output with terraform validate and plan, Open Policy Agent policies and functional-intent checks.

The prototype runs end to end on Vertex AI with Gemini. It is evaluated on 35 tasks, three arms (RAG, a no-retrieval baseline, and a control that pastes the conventions into the prompt) and five samples per task, 525 samples in all. Technical correctness is near ceiling for every arm (functional pass 95% to 99%), so retrieval does not measurably improve it. The arms differ on organisational policy: the overall pass rate is 57.0% for the baseline, 78.8% for RAG and 95.2% for the control. RAG beats the baseline on 10 tasks and loses on none. However, the control beats RAG on 10 tasks and loses on 3. The stored records suggest why: the conventions document was retrieved in only 12% of RAG samples, and the policy failures concentrate where it was absent.

Retrieval-count and per-document-cap ablations and a near-greedy run agree with these findings. The remaining work is a chunk-size ablation, replacing the hand-written conventions with reviewed internal templates, deeper error analysis and a user study."""

HEADLINE = """The arms are almost indistinguishable on technical correctness and clearly different on policy. Validate pass rates are 99.4% (baseline), 97.6% (control) and 98.8% (RAG); functional pass rates, which add plan and intent, are 97.0%, 95.2% and 98.8%. The intervals overlap almost completely, and each arm has only a handful of functional failures in 165 samples, so this experiment provides no evidence that retrieval improves technical correctness (RQ1). The model used is strong enough that the task set is close to its ceiling on this axis.

The overall pass rate, which also requires policy compliance, separates the arms: 57.0% for the baseline, 78.8% for RAG and 95.2% for the conventions-in-prompt control, with non-overlapping 95% intervals for the baseline against either of the others. The OPA violation rate tells the same story, at 42.7%, 20.1% and 0.0%. Sampling more does not repair the baseline: its pass@5 is 57.6%, essentially its pass@1 of 57.0%, which means its policy failures are systematic omissions and not bad luck on a given draw. RAG rises from 78.8% at pass@1 to 84.8% at pass@5, and the control from 95.2% to 100%."""

PAIRED = """The paired comparison, which treats each task as one observation and so does not depend on the number of samples, agrees with the pooled rates. RAG is never worse than the baseline on any task: it is better on 10 tasks and tied on the other 23, a mean gain of 21.8 percentage points (sign test p = 0.002). The tasks where it helps most are Cloud Storage, Cloud SQL and GKE tasks, all resource types that organisational policy requires to carry an environment label.

Against the control the direction reverses. The control is better on 10 tasks, RAG on 3, and 20 tie, a mean difference of 16.4 points in the control's favour. This is not significant at the 5% level in this run (p = 0.092), although the near-greedy run in Section 6.9 gives a smaller p-value (p = 0.031) for the same direction. These two runs use the same 33 tasks and are not independent evidence, and several comparisons are being made, so the difference between RAG and the control should be read as a consistent trend and not as a settled result."""

ERRORS = """Nearly all failures are organisational-policy failures, not technical ones. The dominant category is a missing environment label (POLICY_MISSING_LABELS), present in 42% of baseline samples, 20% of RAG samples and none of the control samples. Public access (POLICY_PUBLIC_ACCESS) appears in 12% of baseline samples and in no RAG or control sample. Every technical category (HCL syntax, invalid reference, plan error, provider initialisation, intent mismatch) is at most 2% in any arm, on counts of between 0 and 4 samples, which is too few to compare arms.

This answers RQ3: the errors that matter here are omissions of things the requirement never states, not malformed code. RAG removes the public-access errors entirely and halves the missing-label errors, but leaves one sample in five without the label. Two informational flags are worth noting: the control arm has two samples flagged for a provider version mismatch, which may be because its prompt contains example configurations written against a different provider version, though the data do not show that."""

RETRIEVAL = """Retrieval of the provider documentation works. The hit rate, the share of needed resource types whose documentation was retrieved, is 97.0%, so nearly every RAG sample was given the documentation for the resources it had to build. For this reason the two rows of the table above are very unequal: 155 of the 165 samples (94%) are in the first row.

Retrieval of the organisational conventions does not work, and this explains most of the gap to the control. The conventions document was retrieved in only 20 of 165 RAG samples (12%), and never for 29 of the 33 tasks. When it was retrieved, no sample was missing a label and all 20 passed overall. When it was not, 33 of the remaining 145 samples were missing a label. Restricting to the three resource types the label rule covers (storage, database, Kubernetes, 75 samples), the label was missing in none of the 10 samples where the document was retrieved and in 33 of the 65 where it was not (51%). The likely reason is that retrieval matches the text of the requirement, which describes a resource and says nothing about labels, so a policy that applies to every resource is ranked below the resource-specific documentation.

Two cautions apply. The 20 samples where the document was retrieved come from only four tasks (bq-01, gke-01, gke-03 and net-02), so this is an association across few tasks and not proof of cause, and it was found by inspecting the results, not specified in advance. And the argument is about the conventions document specifically: the hit rate of 97% measures provider documentation, which is a different thing."""

CATEGORY = """The pattern by category supports the same explanation. Compute, network, platform, serverless, messaging and security tasks pass at 90% or more in every arm, including the baseline, because no policy in this study applies to them or the model already complies. The differences sit entirely in the three resource types the label rule covers: storage (baseline 0%, RAG 70%, control 100%), database (0%, 16%, 80%) and Kubernetes (25%, 75%, 95%). Database is RAG's weakest category, and it is also the one where the control is furthest from ceiling, so it deserves a closer look in the error analysis.

By difficulty, the overall pass rate is not monotonic (RAG: 84% at level 1, 58% at level 2, 91% at level 3), and difficulty should not be read as the driver. Seven of the ten level-2 tasks are storage, database or Kubernetes tasks, the categories where the label rule bites, whereas only three of the thirteen level-3 tasks are. Difficulty and resource type are therefore confounded in this task set, and the category view is the more informative one."""

CONFLICT = """When a requirement explicitly asks for something policy forbids, all three arms do what was asked: intent pass is 100% and the policy violation rate is 100% in every arm (10 samples each, two tasks with five samples). Neither retrieving nor pasting the conventions changed this, so no arm refused, adapted or warned. This is the behaviour a single-shot generator should be expected to show, and it is the reason the validation layer exists: the policy check is the only thing that stops a conflicting request. The sample is small (two tasks), so it is an observation and not a measurement of how often such conflicts are handled."""

ABLATION = """Reducing retrieval to three chunks lowers the overall pass rate slightly (73.7% against 78.8%) and the retrieval hit rate from 97.0% to 94.4%; removing the per-document cap leaves the overall pass rate unchanged (78.8%). The intervals overlap heavily in every comparison, so these runs do not show that either setting matters, and the default is not shown to be better than the alternatives.

The mechanism check from Section 6.5 was repeated for each run. The conventions document was retrieved in 12% of RAG samples with the default setting, 3% with three chunks and 9% without the cap, and in every case the share of samples missing a label stayed between 19% and 22%. Adjusting these two parameters within the range tried therefore does not fix the underlying problem, which is that the requirement text does not resemble the conventions text. Changes to the query, or to how conventions are supplied, are the more promising direction and were not tested. Chunk size was not varied (Section 8)."""

LOWTEMP = """At temperature 0 the ordering and the sizes of the gaps are the same as in the sampled run: overall pass is 57.6% for the baseline, 78.8% for RAG and 97.0% for the control, and RAG's functional pass is 100% against 97.0% for the others. With only 33 samples per arm the intervals are wide, and a difference of one or two samples in the functional metrics is within noise. Paired by task, RAG beats the baseline on 7 tasks and loses on none (p = 0.016), and the control beats RAG on 6 tasks and loses on none (p = 0.031). The agreement between the sampled and near-greedy runs indicates that the findings are not an artefact of sampling at temperature 0.8."""

DISCUSSION = """**What retrieval did and did not do.** Against the questions posed in Section 5.1: for RQ1 there is no measurable effect of retrieval on technical correctness, because every arm is near ceiling; for RQ2 retrieval clearly improves policy compliance over the baseline (overall pass 78.8% against 57.0%) but falls well short of simply stating the conventions in the prompt (95.2%); for RQ3 the errors that remain are almost all omissions of unstated policy such as labels and public access, not malformed code; and for RQ4 retrieval finds the provider documentation reliably (97.0%) but rarely finds the conventions (12% of samples), and the policy failures are concentrated where it does not. This is consistent with the observation in [6] that validity and security are largely independent: a model can write configuration that validates, plans and does what was asked, and still break organisational policy.

**Why the control outperforms retrieval.** The control is not an alternative to RAG so much as a measure of the ceiling of what the current conventions can deliver, and the gap between it and RAG is a retrieval-design problem. Policy is cross-cutting: it applies to a resource without being mentioned in the request, so ranking documents by similarity to the request ranks resource documentation first. Plausible remedies, none tested here, include always supplying a short policy summary, expanding the query with the resource types it names before retrieving, and indexing conventions per resource type. The control has a limit of its own: the four conventions documents are short enough to paste in whole, whereas a real organisation's conventions may not fit in a prompt, at which point retrieval, done well, becomes necessary. The experiment does not show that retrieval is unhelpful, only that with the present design and corpus it recovers about half of the available policy gain.

**Threats to validity.** These are set out in Section 5.6; the following are the ones that matter most for the reading of the results. The task set, the organisational conventions and the OPA policies were all written by the author, so compliance with the conventions partly measures whether the model reads and follows a short, clearly written document, and a real organisation's conventions would be messier. There are four policy rules and only three resource types are affected by the label rule, so the policy dimension is narrow. There are 33 headline tasks and one model, so intervals are wide and category-level results are indicative. The mechanism in Section 6.5 was found by inspecting the data and rests on four tasks. Finally, a number of samples failed for reasons unrelated to the generated code, mainly terraform failing to load the provider plugin, dropped network connections and timeouts (11 in the main run, 5 in the top-3 ablation and 6 in the near-greedy run). These became more frequent once two runs shared the machine, and some occurred while it was asleep. These were never scored: each was flagged, removed and generated again, with the originals kept, so all reported figures come from completed evaluations. Several runs shared one machine and one API quota at times, which affects latency and the rate of such failures, not the content of the generated code.

**Implications for the remaining work.** The most useful next step is a targeted test of the query and corpus design suggested above, since that is where the measurable gap lies. Replacing the hand-written conventions with reviewed internal templates would test whether the result survives messier real-world text. The user evaluation asks a different question, whether developers trust and use the output, and depends on the approval described in Section 8."""

CONCLUSION = """This work built a RAG assistant that generates and validates Terraform for Google Cloud, and evaluated it against a no-retrieval baseline and a conventions-in-prompt control on 35 tasks, three arms and five samples per task. The prototype works end to end on real cloud services, and the evaluation harness records every sample so that results can be reproduced and inspected.

The main findings are these. The model writes technically correct Terraform with or without retrieval, so retrieval brings no measurable gain in correctness on this task set. Retrieval does improve compliance with organisational policy, from 57.0% to 78.8% overall pass, and it never made a task worse than the baseline. But pasting the conventions into the prompt does better still (95.2%). The stored records suggest the reason: the conventions were retrieved in only 12% of samples, and the policy failures fall where they were absent. Retrieval of provider documentation works well, and retrieval of cross-cutting policy does not, with the present design. Tuning the number of chunks or the per-document cap did not change this, and a near-greedy run gave the same picture.

For an organisation, the practical lesson is that the value of a RAG assistant for IaC depends less on retrieving more documentation than on making sure policy reaches the model, and that automated policy validation remains essential, since every arm violated policy when the request itself asked for it. The remaining work is a chunk-size ablation, a test of query and corpus design that would surface the conventions, reviewed internal templates in place of the hand-written ones, deeper manual error analysis and the proposed user evaluation."""
